            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, the air leakage for the age of the home is fairly reasonable, but there is certainly room for improvement.

Air leakage was observed through the weather stripping along the exterior doors. I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was observed around pipe penetrations within the boiler room. This includes the penetrations for the water tank fill pipes and HRV intake and exhuats pipes. 

Although minimal, there was some air leakage observed coming through the rangehood in the kitchen, indicating the damper may not be properly sealing when not in use. When it comes time to replace this, I recommend to consider an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. As the HRV exhaust is connected to the bathroom, you could consider removing the bathroom fan altogether. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 60 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ. 

</airSeal>
                <HeatingSystem>---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BOILER

Your current oil-fired boiler was originally rated at 87% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Please note, the upgrade savings below also incorporates using the boiler to heat your domestic hot water. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, and continuing to use this boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $ 1,000 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DOMESTIC HOT WATER (WITH CURRENT BOILER)

In addition to the Flamecor unit connected to the boiler, you also have a 182 litre electric hot water tank which you mentioned was used during the summer months (while the boiler was shut down). Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have a very efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water, year round, via the dedicated loop connected to the 'Flamecor' unit. This can be done by turning down all the thermostats within the home during the summer. 



Savings:

By using your boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
